            "text": "At the edge of a small town, there was a library that opened before sunrise and closed only when the last reader remembered to leave. Its windows were tall, dusty, and always warm with light. People said the building had no special magic, yet everyone who entered carried out something they had not brought in.\n\nA baker found a map of distant rivers and began naming her breads after ports she hoped to visit. A mechanic discovered poems tucked inside a manual on engines; afterward, he listened more carefully to the rhythms of machines. Children arrived looking for dragons, detectives, or disasters, and departed with quieter treasures: questions.\n\nThe librarian, Mara, believed questions were the most useful things a person could own. Answers could be stored, repeated, framed, or forgotten. Questions moved. They followed people home, sat beside their beds, interrupted boring meetings, and sometimes persuaded them to change their lives.\n\nEach evening, Mara walked between the shelves and straightened the books. She did not mind the silence. It was not empty silence. It held the murmur of thousands of voices waiting patiently behind their covers. Outside, buses sighed at the curb, dogs barked behind fences, and kitchen windows glowed across the street.\n\nOne winter night, a boy asked Mara whether every book had an ending. She considered the snow gathering on the steps.\n\n\u201cEvery book does,\u201d she said. \u201cBut not every story.\u201d\n\nThe boy nodded as if he had been given a key. He chose a novel, then ran."
